<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Hiroshima Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Hiroshima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A large domain on the Inland Sea, ruled by the Asano family from Hiroshima castle for two and a half centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the Mori were reduced to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sekigahara">Sekigahara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave Aki and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bingo-province">Bingo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukushima-masanori">Fukushima Masanori</a> in 1600. In 1619 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> dismissed Masanori for repairing the flood-damaged castle without permission, and transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asano-nagaakira">Asano Nagaakira</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> with about 426,000 koku.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asano-clan">Asano</a> ruled the domain until its abolition. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a>, built on the delta of the Ota river, grew into a major castle town and port. The domain profited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, salt pans, oyster farming in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-bay">Hiroshima Bay</a> and paper, and controlled the shrine island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima">Itsukushima</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shukkeien">Shukkeien</a> garden was laid out for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asano-nagaakira">Nagaakira</a> from 1620.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> served as a base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a>'s expeditions against neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Choshu</a> in the 1860s but sought mediation and joined the imperial side in 1868. After abolition in 1871 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima-prefecture">Hiroshima Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hiroshima-domain.jpg" alt="Hiroshima Domain" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Evelyn-rose · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hiroshima-Castle-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hiroshima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fukushima Masanori, Asano Nagaakira, Asano Nagakoto</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jodo Shinshu Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Aki Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=hiroshima-domain" aria-label="Hiroshima Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8048 932 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hiroshima Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8368 1188L8530 1145M8368 1188L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8368 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8368 1188h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hiroshima. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=hiroshima-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
